  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n\nWe propose a population-based genetic algorithm approach to explore the solution space more thoroughly, aiming to escape local minima and find a better C\u2085 value. This method maintains a population of candidate solutions, each satisfying constraints, and evolves them through mutation and selection based on fitness.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n\nThe problem is to find h \u2208 [0,1]^n such that \u222bh = 1 and minimize max_k \u222bh(x)(1 - h(x+k))dx. This corresponds to minimizing the maximum cross-correlation across all lags. We represent h as an array of n_points, with n=70 initially. Convert the problem into an unconstrained optimization using fitness functions based on the overlap integral.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n\n1. Initialize population of size pop_size with random h arrays meeting sum=1 and h[i] \u2208 [0,1].\n2. Mutation: Add Gaussian noise to each parent and project onto feasible region.\n3. Crossover: Blend two parents' vectors.\n4. Evaluate fitness for each candidate using FFT-based convolution.\n5. Select top candidates for next generation based on fitness.\n6. Terminate when budget or improvement threshold is reached.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n\nPopulation size: 25 initial. Mutation rate: 0.05. Crossover: blending. Selection: elitist.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n\nEstimate per-iteration cost including FFT evaluations. Monitor time and generate new candidates accordingly.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n\nEnsure all candidates meet constraints and validate C\u2085 through evaluator.",
